<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Final War of the Roman Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">32–<a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as War of Actium · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Final War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> was the civil war of 32–30 BCE between Octavian and the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Octavian's fleet under Agrippa won the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> in 31 BCE, and Antony and Cleopatra took their own lives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 30 BCE. Egypt was annexed, and Octavian, named Augustus in 27 BCE, became the first Roman emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/final-war-of-the-roman-republic.jpg" alt="Final War of the Roman Republic" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laureys a Castro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castro_Battle_of_Actium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s assassination, Octavian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Marcus Lepidus formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>. They defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a> in 42 BCE, and Lepidus was expelled in 36 BCE. Octavian then held the west, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, Italia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and Antony the east, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Antony, married since 40 BCE to Octavia, Octavian's sister, took up with Cleopatra, and Octavian had his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas">Gaius Maecenas</a> produce a propaganda campaign against him. Antony's Donations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> gave much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s eastern territory to Cleopatra and her children. Octavian then seized Antony's will from the Temple of Vesta, and its terms, including Antony's wish to be buried at Alexandria, angered the senate. In 32 BCE the senate declared war on Cleopatra. Both consuls and forty percent of the senate left Rome to join Antony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Both sides raised armies of almost 200,000 men. Octavian's chief adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> commanded his fleet of 350 smaller, more manoeuvrable ships with experienced crews, while Antony brought 290 larger ships with inexperienced crews. Agrippa disrupted Antony's supply lines and took the port of Methone. On 2 September 31 BCE, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a>, Antony's fleet was drawn out from the shore and overcome. Cleopatra's squadron slipped through a gap in the line and Antony followed, but most of the fleet was sunk, and the pair retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> with about 90 ships. Antony's land commanders surrendered their legions after a week.</p><p>In early 30 BCE Octavian marched by land through Asia. Lucius Pinarius, whom Antony hoped would supply an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, had changed sides. Octavian placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under siege, Antony's remaining cavalry and fleet surrendered, and on 1 August 30 BCE Antony fell on his sword. According to the ancient accounts he died in Cleopatra's arms in her mausoleum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Cleopatra bargained for her children's lives, but Octavian refused to spare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> she died from a snake bite or a poisonous ointment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Octavian, who executed Antony's supporters, ended a century of civil wars. In 27 BCE the senate named him Augustus and gave him unprecedented powers.</p><p>Augustus passed laws that kept the appearance of the republic while making his own position paramount, and ruled as the first Roman emperor. The critical border provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> came under his direct rule. His reign opened a period of internal stability later called the Pax Romana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of Actium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
